rsedit -- timing report (wall clock, machine-dependent)
=======================================================

These operations run inside Rust and are never driven by the
interpreter, so no fuel is charged and there is nothing exact to
count. What follows is wall clock, and wall clock describes the
machine as much as the code.

Two things make it useful anyway. Deltas compare against the previous
run in the same environment, so a change of direction is visible. And
every figure is also given as a multiple of a calibration workload
timed in the same run, so a machine-wide slowdown can be told apart
from a real regression.

Only complexity classes are asserted on here. Absolute durations are
tracked, never used as a pass/fail threshold: a wall-clock bound
encodes the speed of whatever machine wrote it, and goes red on a
slower one while staying green through a genuine regression on a
faster one.  reference: one calibration round takes 4.493 ns on this
machine; the 'x ref' column is each cost divided by that.
environment: x86_64-linux debug 16-cpu baseline: the previous run in
this same environment, so the deltas below are meaningful.  Durations
are corrected for how fast the machine was on each run before their
delta is taken, so a busy machine shows up as noise on the calibration
row instead of as a regression on every other one. Ratios need no
correction; they cancel it already.

1. CALIBRATION -------------- A fixed integer workload, timed the same
way as everything below. It is the control: if a row got slower and
this did not, the code got slower; if this moved too, the machine did.
reference round 4.493 ns (+0.0% noise) the unit the 'x ref' column is
in

2. PARSER --------- Source text to AST. Not fuel-charged -- nothing is
evaluating yet -- so this is one of the few interpreter costs that
only a clock can see.  parse one form 4943 ns (-1.1% noise) 1100 x ref
doubling the source 2.01x (+0.1% noise) linear is ~2x; must stay under
3x

3. SCOPE CHAIN -------------- The same loop run at the top level and
inside 12 nested `let`s. Lookup walks the environment chain by name,
so the gap between these two is what nesting costs. Lexical addressing
would collapse it toward 1.00x.  eval step at depth 0 2838 ns (-3.5%
noise) 632 x ref depth 12 vs depth 0 4.06x (-0.4% noise) must stay
under 12x

4. GAP BUFFER ------------- Insertion at the cursor, and reading a
viewport out. The locality row is the one number in this file that is
a known problem rather than a control: it is what "linear scan from
character zero" looks like at 100,000 lines. The bound below is not
asking the operation to be fast, only to stay linear -- anything
quadratic overshoots it by orders of magnitude. If a cached line index
lands, this collapses toward 1.0x and the bound should be tightened
hard to lock the win in.  insert one char 10.5 ns (+1.6% noise) 2.34 x
ref doubling characters typed 2.02x (+0.9% noise) linear is ~2x; must
stay under 3x read 50 lines at head 38073 ns (-0.4% noise) one
viewport read 50 lines at line 50000 21052444 ns (-0.8% noise) one
viewport mid-file vs head 553x (-0.4% noise) linear at this size; must
stay under 2500x

5. LAYOUT --------- Walking the window tree to compute tiled views,
once per redraw. The growth row asks that per-frame cost not drift
upward as the editor runs.  compose one frame 162428 ns (-0.0% noise)
36148 x ref doubling frames rendered 2.00x (+0.2% noise) linear is
~2x; must stay under 3x

6. COMMAND PATH --------------- Key event, keymap lookup, Lisp
dispatch, buffer write, post-command hook.  The per-keystroke figure
is what a user waits for between pressing a key and seeing the
character; the exact work behind it is the command-path section of the
cost report.  one keystroke, end to end 3104 ns (+1.8% noise) 691 x
ref

7. CONCURRENCY -------------- The same total work done sequentially on
one thread, then spread across three threads writing to three separate
buffers. Per-buffer locks make the concurrent run faster; a single
coarse lock around the buffer table would make it *slower* than
sequential, since every write would serialise anyway and pay
contention on top. Three threads on separate buffers should approach
0.33x; 1.00x is the line between fine-grained and coarse.  one buffer
write 54 ns (-2.5% noise) sequential 3 threads vs sequential 0.61x
(+17.9% SLOWER) under 1.00x means the locks are fine-grained

8. EXECUTION BUDGET ------------------- A runaway loop calling an O(n)
primitive on a long list, under a deliberately small budget. The
budget's promise is about elapsed time, so this is the one row in the
suite where an absolute duration is the thing being asserted.  stop
(while t (length lst)) over 20000 elements 2.47 ms (+1.0% noise) 60000
units of budget; must stop under 1000 ms

